## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design an online, latency-constrained text classifier with limited features and simple drift checks, testing hashing-based features, SGD/partial_fit, and streaming evaluation.

## Key Concepts
- Hashing-based text features (30 features)
- Online learning with partial_fit
- Latency constraints on CPU
- Drift checks and calibration
- Evaluation metrics for spam detection (precision, recall, F1, PR-AUC)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple online logistic regression with partial_fit (conceptual)
class OnlineLR {
  constructor(nFeatures){ this.w = new Float64Array(nFeatures); this.b = 0; }
  predict(x){ let s=this.b; for(let i=0;i<x.length;i++) s+= this.w[i]*x[i]; return 1/(1+Math.exp(-s)); }
  partial_fit(x,y,lr=0.1){ const p=this.predict(x); const g=y-p; for(let i=0;i<x.length;i++) this.w[i]+=lr*g*x[i]; this.b+=lr*g; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multiclass spam types (phishing, promo, etc.)?
- What changes if label latency increases from seconds to minutes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical anomaly detection with limited labels, feature engineering across multiple sensors, and edge deployment constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Unsupervised/semi-supervised anomaly detection
- Windowing and rolling statistics
- Feature scaling and imputation
- Edge-friendly model size and latency

## Code Example

```javascript
// Baseline anomaly detector: simple z-score on rolling means
function rollingStats(values, w){
  const means=[]; const stds=[];
  for(let i=0;i<values.length;i++){
    const start=Math.max(0,i-w+1);
    const window = values.slice(start,i+1);
    const m = window.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)/window.length;
    const v = window.reduce((acc,v)=>acc+(v-m)*(v-m),0)/(window.length);
    means[i]=m; stds[i]=Math.sqrt(v);
  }
  return {means,stds};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle concept drift in this setup?
- How would you validate results in production with streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical, beginner-friendly ML pipeline for churn with real-world constraints: leakage prevention, encoding choices, and model comparisons that balance performance and interpretability.

## Key Concepts

- Data leakage prevention via time-based or grouped splits
- Handling class imbalance in binary targets
- Baseline vs tree-based models and interpretability
- Evaluation metrics: AUROC andPR-AUC, plus feature importance tools like SHAP

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo baseline pipeline (illustrative only)
function trainBaseline(data){
  const X = oneHotEncode(data.features);
  const {train, test} = timeSplit(X, data.labels);
  const model = trainLR(train.features, train.labels, {C:1.0});
  const preds = model.predict(test.features);
  return evaluate(test.labels, preds);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a much larger dataset?
- What monitoring would you set up to detect data drift over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to design scalable, tenant-aware ML pipelines with calibration, streaming features, drift handling, and safe rollout strategies across enterprise-scale tenants.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant feature stores and leakage prevention
- Shared base model + per-tenant calibrator
- Real-time features from streaming usage and billing
- Online retraining with drift detectors
- Offline + online evaluation and staged rollout

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo calibration hook
function calibrate(probs, tenantId, store){
  const params = store.getParams(tenantId)
  // adjust by temp scaler
  return Math.min(0.999, Math.max(0.001, probs * params.scale + params.bias))
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate per-tenant calibration offline and online?
- How would you guard against cross-tenant data leakage in CV and feature drift?
